From: PowOS <noreply@powback.com>
Subject: [PATCH] backends/anland: KWin windows as their own Android tasks

A window is exported, not moved: it stays a normal KWin window and is drawn - with its popups - into the buffers of
an Android task's surface (AnlandWindowTask, anland_window_task.cpp), the way a window screencast draws it, when it
changes. While the task shows it the window lives on a virtual desktop of its own ("Android tasks": the desktop
neither paints it nor sends it input, while it can keep the focus) and renders offscreen (not suspended, frame
callbacks keep coming); its size follows the task. Touch from the task goes straight to the window or popup under
the finger; keys go through KWin's keyboard to the focused window, which the task's focus makes this one.

The display daemon pairs a consumer and a producer per surface id (the HELLO's payload; none = the desktop) and
relays control lines between KWin and the PowOS app: the user's maximize (title bar, menu, shortcut - patch 0014's
hook) or D-Bus org.powos.WindowTasks.show(windowId) sends "open", the app starts the task; its "closed", or maximize
again, puts the window back on the desktop. Activating an exported window the desktop way (the taskbar) brings its
task to the front instead of switching to its desktop.
The producer's handshake and fd pickup get non-blocking variants for that (connect_to_deamon_begin/_finish,
request_consumer_fds/finish_exit_fallback), and the dmabuf import is shared with the desktop's layer.
